g through  the five stages of grief and it was you  know it was a significant effort there  was a lot of debate at the time about  like is this important and shouldn't we  be adding a bunch of new features and  you know look at hotmail and look at  yahoo mail and etc but we spent the  effort to improve the client and to  improve the user experience for people  who didn't have the latest laptop and  didn't have the really fast Wi-Fi and so  on and it made this enormous difference  in actual adoption so for me that  cemented the lesson for forever right  measure the user experience even though  it's painful even though it's gonna take  you know a bunch of big engineering  projects because you will never build  your app the same way again  and you will get a much better user  adoption as a result of doing it much  more impactful than any feature that we  could have launched and yes it's hard  right so the engineering team will also  have to go through the five stages of  grief  in dealing with the fact that in  addition to writing all the features  they also need to write the things that  let you measure what the users are  seeing but persist metric number two  tail latency alright sounds incredibly  boring unless you're in reliability in  which case you know this is we live for  this stuff but what's let's run with  boring for right now right so quick  survey of the room hands up if you think  roughly you know how reliable your  service is ah see I gave way too much  away like the only people who are  raising their hands are the ones who  clearly do not know  because because they're in my team and  they already know what this drill is  right so yes we just talked about the  importance of actually measuring the  user experience the way the users  experience it but if you do that then  clearly you know how often the user gets  an error and therefore you know how  often your user considers your service  to be available if that describe you and  your perspective on your service right  now this will come as something of a  disappointment right so users typically  treat a sufficiently slow response as  the same thing as an error and they'll  do one of two things they'll hit refresh  if they're kind and think that otherwise  highly think of your app or they'll  switch to a different app and maybe  they'll come back to yours but certainly  they won't have a good experience and it  will be the same for them as if they had  gotten an error except the error at  least is sort of a polite prompt that  says you should hit refresh whereas no  answer is impolite and tells you to you  know waste your time and so I think of  availability as being the above formula  right availability is a hundred percent  - all of the errors that you return and  also - all of the slow responses you  give and you know if you picture this  for yourself right suppose you use a  nice snappy modern web based application  or mobile based application we offer a  few of them ourselves and on average you  know you sort of make a query and within  half a second the thing responds and  actually gives you the answer or does  the transition who I'd ever find that's  great right  so suppose one time in 30 whatever it  was that it was doing to process that  fails somewhere internally in your stack  and so eventually you know your timeouts  kick in and you assume you retry the  query for them as opposed to making them  hit refresh because you know that's  that's barbaric  but so you refresh after 10 seconds and  it retries ok so your availability  metrics there right we'll say that it's  99.9 something available because that's  how many errors you're serving your  users will say it's about 97% available  which is for a mobile app not terrible  but it's not great right most of the  mobile apps that I use they're probably  on the order of 99 percent available  I can definitely notice as a user the  difference between let's say 99.5 and 97  which is what I have here you'd have a  completely different view of what the  user experiences just by changing the  way that you consider availability and  so then the question of course becomes  well how much slower than median does  the thing have to be before the user  will consider it to be an error I don't  have a generic answer for you because it  depends a lot on your app right if  you're using an app like mail and it  takes a few extra seconds for a mail to  be received you don't even notice right  if it takes a few extra seconds for you  to hit Send before the mail is  registered as being sent you notice that  right and if you click on a mail and the  mail doesn't snap up instantly you're  going to retry it yourself in a couple  of seconds so maybe factor four but you  can probably see this and if you can't  you should figure it out right again  back to measuring the user experience  because you want to know at what point  your user thinks your app isn't working  correctly and if you don't know that  well your users do and you won't change  your app appropriately and then you'll  have less adoption and you know  eventually catastrophe so if you want a  rule of thumb write something to walk  away with 5 X 5 X is a pretty good rule  of thumb for many interactive  applications that I see people using and  it's not complicated at that point right  you can measure what your median latency  is just consider anything that's more  than five times as slow as your median  latency to be an error and you know  again five stages of grief right  followed by then you'll actually spend  some time and try to figure out really  is it five X or is it more like 8 X  right I encourage you not to be overly  optimistic because again you know  eventually the users will make their own  choice about your application you'd  really like to know what they're seeing  and how they're behaving and you'd like  to have the opportunity to tune your app  to deal with that but figure out what  latency you know starts to cause peopl