n a cloud product  that makes you buy stuff in advance to  get a good price how many you're gonna  have to purchase and and you're done  right easy peasy  well so you know unfortunately because  there is this extra bit which is that  you're all either developers in this  room or you manage developers for the  most part all of you are doing things to  that binary that's running on those  servers and you're doing things that  change the amount of time that is  required for processing a given query  right and so even if you are perfect at  extrapolating how many queries you're  going to be getting you're changing the  if you will the denominator and  sometimes you're not changing it by a  little bit right it was not uncommon for  us to see swings of 50 percent over the  course of a week  if you're not watching it because it's  easy to sort of add things to the inner  loop and it's easy to add a feature that  makes a call out to something else in  blah blah blah and even small changes  like configuration file changes can  sometimes dramatically change the amount  of compute capacity that's required to  process a query and we call that service  efficiency overall right how many CPU  cycles disk spindle seeks you know  Network bytes etc etc is required to  answer a query or to serve an  interesting action in your service and  like I said easy to change basically  invisible right unless you do  performance testing all the time which  we'll get to in a second and one of the  most frequent flyers in the post-mortem  that follows inevitably from this right  because of course what happens with  service efficiency is none of you  presumably run your services at the very  hairy edge of falling over all the time  right everyone builds redundancy into  how they do provision and either it's  redundancy to handle Peaks that happen  to happen right you're running a new  service and something interesting  happens in the world you can use your  own imagination this is what that might  