ntly right the best practice  of course would be we'll build a system  that actually measures it for me and  spits out a report every night and then  I'll notice immediately it's much easier  to automate this sort of thing and have  a performance test and you don't have to  test every little edge case right you  just have to test the things that are  the big volume drivers for your service  but if you have it automated and you get  this report every night you'll notice  you'll also notice other interesting  things like hey you know our service  efficient still the same but our latency  has been creeping up a few milliseconds  every week right and so we've gone from  having a service that was nice and  snappy to a service that's kind of  kludgy even on a good device and really  bad on a bad device over the course of  three months without anything dramatic  happening right and that's obviously  something else that can bite you it  bites you mostly and that your user  adoption seems to know is over a bit  more than you would have hoped you know  if you're running hypothetically on a  cloud provider that just has flexible  use and so you can sort of use more  capacity you might not have a cascade  failure when your service efficiency  gets bad but will happen is your finance  person will come and find you a few  weeks later and they'll say huh you know  we got a bill from our cloud provider  and it's about 30% higher than we had  expected what's going on right and then  I would say typically two or three weeks  of thrashing goes on you accuse the  cloud provider of over billing you the  cloud provider you know does they run  Diagnostics that seems to be running  normally this is assuming you have a  cloud provider who lets you flex in and  out right if you have one where you're  running on fixed instances in your  service just goes down which has the  upside of no extra bill and you know  sort of prompt diagnosis so I guess you  know there's there's pros and cons to  ea